<section data-analytics-location="hero" class="subhero wrap" aria-labelledby="page-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">ILLUSTRATIVE KUBERNETES HANDOFF PACKAGE</p><h1 id="page-title">A platform you can<br /><em>understand and operate.</em></h1><p class="subhero-lede">The build is only part of the result. An implementation should leave a clear record of what was designed, how it is operated, and what was actually tested.</p><div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="{{ landing.bookingUrl }}" data-booking>Discuss an implementation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="{{ 'Deliverables question' | contactHref(page.url) }}">Email a deliverables question <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div><aside class="subhero-note"><span class="eyebrow">SAMPLE CONTENT</span><strong>Architecture.<br />Runbook.<br />Validation.</strong><span aria-hidden="true">✳</span></aside></section>

<section data-analytics-location="examples" class="section journey-section" aria-labelledby="sample-note-title"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">WHAT THIS PAGE IS</p><h2 id="sample-note-title">A preview of the form.<br /><em>Not a client case study.</em></h2></div><p class="heading-aside">The excerpts below are illustrative examples for a small, single-site private application platform. They are not evidence of a completed deployment or a claim that any test has passed.</p></div><div class="sample-index"><a href="#architecture">01 / Architecture <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="#runbook">02 / Operating runbook <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="#validation">03 / Validation record <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>

<section data-analytics-location="architecture" class="section wrap sample-section" id="architecture" aria-labelledby="architecture-title"><div class="sample-heading"><span class="eyebrow">01 / ARCHITECTURE EXCERPT</span><h2 data-interest="architecture" id="architecture-title">Know what exists<br /><em>and who owns it.</em></h2><p>A real architecture record identifies components, dependencies, boundaries, and ownership. This simplified diagram shows the kind of relationship a handoff should explain.</p></div><div class="sample-sheet"><div class="sample-sheet-top"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE / NOT A CLIENT SYSTEM</span><span>ARCHITECTURE VIEW</span></div><div class="architecture-flow" aria-label="Illustrative flow from team access through private platform to application and backups"><div>Team access</div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div>Private network</div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div>Kubernetes platform</div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div>Application + data</div><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><div>Backup target</div></div><p><strong>Ownership note:</strong> The customer owns the hardware, accounts, configuration, and documentation. The final record would identify named operators, access methods, dependencies, and recovery objectives.</p></div></section>

<section data-analytics-location="runbook" class="section wrap sample-section" id="runbook" aria-labelledby="runbook-title"><div class="sample-heading"><span class="eyebrow">02 / OPERATING RUNBOOK EXCERPT</span><h2 data-interest="runbook" id="runbook-title">Put routine work<br /><em>in plain view.</em></h2><p>Runbooks turn platform knowledge into repeatable steps for the receiving operator. Each real procedure is adapted to the environment and reviewed during handoff.</p></div><div class="sample-sheet"><div class="sample-sheet-top"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE / NOT A CLIENT PROCEDURE</span><span>PLANNED UPGRADE CHECK</span></div><ol class="sample-steps"><li><strong>Before:</strong> Confirm the change window, platform health, recent backup status, and rollback point.</li><li><strong>During:</strong> Apply the approved change in the agreed order; record versions, commands, and any deviation.</li><li><strong>After:</strong> Verify node readiness, workload health, access, monitoring, and the application owner's checks.</li><li><strong>If validation fails:</strong> Stop the change, follow the documented rollback procedure, and record the outcome.</li></ol></div></section>

<section data-analytics-location="validation" class="section wrap sample-section" id="validation" aria-labelledby="validation-title"><div class="sample-heading"><span class="eyebrow">03 / VALIDATION RECORD EXCERPT</span><h2 data-interest="validation" id="validation-title">Show the evidence.<br /><em>Know the limits.</em></h2><p>A production record states what was tested, when, by whom, against which acceptance criterion, and what remains open. These are example fields, not completed results.</p></div><div class="sample-sheet"><div class="sample-sheet-top"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE / NO TEST RESULT CLAIMED</span><span>BACKUP RESTORE RECORD</span></div><dl class="validation-grid"><div><dt>Test objective</dt><dd>Restore a sample dataset to an isolated environment.</dd></div><div><dt>Acceptance criterion</dt><dd>Application owner confirms the restored sample is readable and complete.</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence to attach</dt><dd>Backup identifier, restore steps, timestamps, checks, and any deviations.</dd></div><div><dt>Result and sign-off</dt><dd>Left blank until an actual test is run and reviewed.</dd></div></dl></div><p class="micro sample-caveat">The complete package for a real engagement depends on the agreed architecture, scope, risks, and operator responsibilities. <a href="/implementation/">See how a private Kubernetes implementation is scoped.</a></p></section>

